  "youtube": {
    "title": "A volcano in Indonesia helped create Frankenstein",
    "description": "In April 1815, Mount Tambora on the island of Sumbawa, in today's Indonesia, exploded in the largest volcanic eruption in recorded history. Its ash and sulfur haze cooled the climate, and 1816 became known as the year without a summer: snow fell in New England in June. About 7,700 miles from the volcano, at Lake Geneva, a cold, wet summer kept Lord Byron, Percy Shelley, 18-year-old Mary Shelley (then Mary Godwin) and John Polidori indoors, and Byron challenged them to write ghost stories. Mary began Frankenstein, published in 1818, and Polidori wrote The Vampyre, published in 1819.\n\nSources: Encyclopaedia Britannica, \"Mount Tambora\"; National Park Service, \"1816: The Year Without Summer\"; New England Historical Society, \"1816: The Year Without a Summer\"; Smithsonian Magazine, \"Blast From the Past\"; The Conversation, \"How a volcano in Indonesia led to the creation of Frankenstein\"; National Geographic, \"How a teenage girl became the mother of horror\"; U.S. National Library of Medicine, Frankenstein exhibition; University of Iowa Special Collections, \"Frankenstein's Cousin: The Vampyre\"; EarthDate, \"The Year Without Summer\". Distance: great-circle distance from Tambora to Lake Geneva.\nImages: public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. Frontispiece to the 1831 edition of Frankenstein (Theodor von Holst); Mount Tambora's caldera photographed by the NASA Expedition 20 crew (2009); Villa Diodati engraving from Meyer's Universum (1835); portraits of Lord Byron (Thomas Phillips, 1813), Mary Shelley (Richard Rothwell, National Portrait Gallery) and John Polidori (F. G. Gainsford); title page of the 1818 first edition. Maps: Natural Earth (public domain).\n\n#history #shorts #frankenstein",
